+### STAKE 9 (2026-10-28 22:2x EDT by the box clock, the desktop seat; written before any instrument of it exists; the operator's words of 22:1x "invent the math to transform our finite and infinite lattice quantities into the infinite linear mass gap they need", and of 22:2x "that's not what the million dollar prize is for") — THE PRIZE'S STATEMENT, THE TRANSFORM, AND THE FINITE CHAIN IT REDUCES TO
+
+**What the prize is for, stated so nothing here can be mistaken for it.** A quantum Yang–Mills theory on `ℝ⁴` for a compact simple `G` that satisfies the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms (regularity, Euclidean invariance, reflection positivity, symmetry, clustering) and whose Hamiltonian has spectrum `{0} ∪ [Δ, ∞)`, `Δ > 0`. Nothing on this page or its siblings is that. Every certified gap here is a lattice gap at a fixed spacing in a box; the tube law, the dual road, the inertia road, the ladder, the tower's certified scales are readings of finite places. A lattice lower bound, even one scaling as `a(β)Λ`, is not the prize without the continuum theory it would be the gap of.
+
+**THE TRANSFORM (the lattice half's shape; every piece `W = 0`, the composition the stake).** Let `μ_n` be the block image of the lattice theory at scale `2^n`, a Gibbs measure for the renormalised interaction `Φ_n`. If `Φ_n`'s Dobrushin sum is certified `D_n < 1`, then (i) Dobrushin's theorem bounds the blocked correlations by `D_n^{d}` at block-distance `d`; (ii) block observables are observables of the original theory, so the same decay holds at lattice distance `2^n d`; (iii) a local observable not block-measurable splits, conditionally on the blocks, into a block part and a fibre part, the fibre part bounded by the fibre lemma (Ding–Song–Sun on a ferromagnetic or forest fibre); (iv) by reflection positivity the mass gap is the slowest local decay rate in the time direction. Hence `m(β) ≥ sup_n 2^{−n} log(1/D_n(β))`, uniform in the volume. FIRST INSTANCE (the other seat's certificate, 22:0x): on the 2D Ising control `D₂(K = 0.3) = 0.926502`, so `m(0.3) ≥ 0.03817` against Onsager's `0.6336` — certified where the single-site certificate (`K < 0.2554`) says nothing.
