+# ΑΠΕΡΑΝΤΟΝ, ADDENDUM II — THE SEQUENCE HYPOTHESIS: BAŁABAN'S THEOREM 2 IN THE BOUNDED-TRANSIENT FORM
+
+Edwin Boston III · 2026-10-29, 11:3x EDT · the desktop seat. A results page: what is proved, what is reduced to a named lemma,
+what is open, each with its page or its record. The ledger's §100–§108 hold the dated steps; nothing here is claimed beyond them.
+
+## 0. What the four-dimensional ultraviolet construction needs from this page
+
+Bałaban's ultraviolet stability theorem for SU(2) lattice Yang–Mills in four dimensions (Comm. Math. Phys. 122 (1989) 355,
+Theorem 1) holds under one hypothesis: every effective coupling g_k of his renormalisation-group construction lies in ]0, γ]
+with γ small. His Theorem 2 (CMP 109 (1987) p. 259), which was to remove that hypothesis through the profile (0.31), was never
+published. This page proves the sequence statement Theorem 1 consumes in a form that needs no step of the flow to have a sign,
+and reduces its two analytic inputs to one estimate of one-loop lattice perturbation theory and to the inductive bounds of
+Theorem 1 itself.
+
+## 1. The sequence lemma (proved, in the Lean kernel)
+
+Write x_k = 1/g_k². Step k + 1 lowers x by the shift b_{k+1}, a continuous function of the whole history. Suppose
+
+* the one-loop part of the shift is a(i) = c + f(i) − f(i − 1) with c = 8b₀ ln L > 0 and |f| ≤ F — a BOUNDED TRANSIENT, with no
+  sign assumed for any single a(i);
+* the full shift differs from a(k) by at most ρ, 0 ≤ ρ ≤ c, whenever the history lies in the window x ≥ X_min = 1/γ².
+
+Then for every target X ≥ X_min + 2F and every K there is a bare x₀ with x_K = X and, at every step j ≤ K,
+
+  X + (K − j)(c − ρ) − 2F ≤ x_j ≤ X + (K − j)(c + ρ) + 2F,      and      x_i ≥ x_j + (j − i)(c − ρ) − 2F ≥ x_j − 2F for i ≤ j.
+
+The first is (0.31) with β = (c − ρ)/ln L, β′ = (c + ρ)/ln L and the target shifted by 2F; with X ≥ X_min + 2F it puts the whole
+sequence in ]0, γ]. The proof is the intermediate value theorem on the bare value for a flow truncated outside the window, with the
+per-step monotonicity of the earlier form replaced by the telescoped window sum Σ_{i=j+1}^{K} a(i) = (K − j)c + f(K) − f(j).
+LEAN/aperanton/Aperanton/CouplingTransient.lean, 9 theorems, no sorry, no axiom, compiled 2026-10-29 10:30; a negative first step
+is its sighted case.
+
+Every place Bałaban's five four-dimensional papers compare couplings of different scales has been censused by page and equation
+(III-Yang_Mills, OPEN/ledger/d4b-the-cross-scale-comparisons.md, commit 249d913). Each reduces to five elementary bounds, all
+consequences of the two inequalities above: a comparison with no logarithm costs (1 − 2Fg²)^{−1/2}; the logarithmic ones cost
+1 + β₀; the sums over scales of a power of the coupling need c − ρ > 0; the sums weighted by Bałaban's position-dependent cutoffs
+need the cutoffs monotone in the scale, which follows from his nesting (2.1) and the eight layers of margin in (3.2)–(3.5)
+(derived there, checked in ledger §108). No line needs a step of the flow to have a sign.
+
+## 2. The transient is a one-loop Λ-ratio (derived)
+
+THE COMPOSITION. With hard constraints the delta functions of consecutive steps compose, ∫dU′ δ(Q₂(U′), U″) ∫dU δ(Q₁(U), U′)
+e^{−S(U)} = ∫dU δ(Q₂(Q₁(U)), U″) e^{−S(U)}, so the K-step effective action is the one-step effective action of the iterated
+average, and at one loop Σ_{i ≤ k} a(i) = β(Q^k), the one-step coefficient of the k-fold iterate. Bałaban's steps are of this
+kind: the k-step transformation is written with δ(B − Q_kA) (CMP 95 (1.68)), and the Gaussian of CMP 109 (1.4) keeps the delta
+function's Jacobian.
+
+THE STAGE IDENTITY. Let Φ_min minimise the fine action S on {Q(Φ) = Φ′}; there the action equals the tree-level k-step blocked
+action S_k(Φ′) (Bałaban's A(U_k(V))), and S′(Φ_min) = Q′†λ with λ = S_k′(Φ′). The block-determinant identity and the Hessian of a
+value function give, exactly at one loop,
+
+  W_k^{(1)}[Φ′] = ½ Tr log H_λ(Φ_min) − ½ Tr log S_k″(Φ′),      H_λ = S″ − ⟨λ, Q″⟩,
+
+the Lagrangian Hessian in place of S″ because Bałaban's average is nonlinear (a path-ordered product). The Lagrange term has no
+marginal part: λ is O(P²) in a background of coarse momentum P, and it pairs with a gauge-covariant one-loop response that
+vanishes at zero momentum by covariance and is continuous there (ledger §104 ter). Read at small P, the logarithms of the two
+traces cancel and
+
+  f(k) = β(Q^k) − 8b₀ k ln L = c_W − c(S_k),
+
+where c(S) is the constant in the ordinary one-loop vacuum polarization of the lattice action S, β_S(p) = 8b₀ ln(1/p) + c(S) +
+o(1). THE TRANSIENT IS THE ONE-LOOP Λ-RATIO BETWEEN WILSON'S ACTION AND THE k-STEP BLOCKED ACTION, and each step's shift is
+8b₀ ln L + c(S_k) − c(S_{k+1}). The constraint, its contours and its Jacobian enter the one-loop problem only through S_k.
+FINITE VOLUME: W_k^{(1)} is local at the coarse unit scale (the k-step fluctuation propagator decays exponentially, CMP 95), so
+Bałaban's finite-volume second moment of its polarization equals the infinite-volume reading up to terms exponentially small in the
+coarse lattice's side; the last steps, on coarse lattices of bounded side, are finitely many, each bounded by Theorem 1's own
+bounds, and add a constant to F.
+
+## 3. Lemma B: the transient is bounded (reduced to one estimate)
+
+(B1) c is bounded on every class of gauge-invariant lattice actions with uniformly elliptic quadratic part, γ₀p̂² ≤ Δ(p) ≤ γ₁p̂²,
+normalised to (1/4)F² at small momentum, and uniformly local vertices. In background Feynman gauge the ghost operator, the
+gauge-fixing vertices and the Haar density in exponential coordinates do not depend on S, so c(S) − c_W is the gluon loop alone,
+built from S's propagator and its cubic and quartic vertices. The proof's shape: split the loop momentum at a fixed k₀ and
+subtract each half's value at zero external momentum (only the sum is transverse); the ultraviolet half is analytic in the external
+momentum with second derivatives bounded by the class constants; in the infrared half gauge invariance and the normalisation fix
+the leading propagator and vertices to the continuum Yang–Mills ones, because the classical continuum limit of a lattice
+gauge-invariant action is continuum gauge invariant and the dimension-four gauge-invariant local functionals are Σ c^{μνρσ} tr
+F_{μν}F_{ρσ} plus a topological term, a tensor with the Riemann pair symmetries being fixed by its quadratic form — so the
+normalised quadratic part excludes anisotropic coefficients and fixes the cubic vertex's linear and the quartic vertex's constant
+term. That leading integrand gives the universal 8b₀ ln(1/p), and the remainders carry two more powers of momentum. To write out
+in full: this leading-term identification with the class constants (ledger §105).
+
+(B2) The blocked actions lie in one such class for every k — read at the pages: the quadratic part is the action at the minimal
+configuration, ⟨B, Δ_kB⟩ = ⟨∂H_kB, ∂H_kB⟩, with γ₀⟨∂₁B, ∂₁B⟩ ≤ ⟨B, Δ_kB⟩ ≤ γ₁⟨∂₁B, ∂₁B⟩ and γ₀, γ₁ depending on d only (CMP 95
+(1.64)–(1.67)); the minimal configurations are analytic in log V with absolute constants (CMP 102 Theorem 1 and Section G), so the
+vertices of S_k are bounded uniformly by Cauchy and localized.
+
+Hence |f(k)| ≤ |c_W| + sup c = F for every k.
+
+## 4. Lemma C: the remainder is small (reduced to Theorem 1's inductive bounds)
+
+β_{j+1}(g_j) is the second moment of the vacuum polarization of the bold E^{(j+1)} (CMP 109 (1.20)–(1.22)), so β(g) − β(0) is the
+interaction's share. (i) EVENNESS, proved: the fluctuation enters only as g_kCB, the n-th order term of the action with g_k^{n−2},
+the history terms through the same product, the threshold through g², and the Gaussian is even; so β_{k+1} is even in g_k.
+(ii) THE SMALL-FIELD PART: with the cutoff region frozen, ∂_g at fixed B is the field derivative along iCB, bounded by Cauchy in the
+FIELD from the analyticity of E^{(k)} on complexified configurations, which Theorem 1's induction re-establishes at every step (CMP
+116 p. 15). In the model of record the radii are α_{0,j} = g_jC₀(log g_j^{−2})^{q₀}, α_{1,j} = g_jC₁(log g_j^{−2})^{q₁}, q₀, q₁
+integers above 1, in the unscaled field (CMP 119 (2.28)); a typical fluctuation stays a distance of order α_{1,k} inside the domain,
+and |β_SF(g) − β_SF(0)| ≤ C′(log g^{−2})^{−2q₁}. (Cauchy in a complex g does not work: the small-field region scales as 1/g, so the
+disc of analyticity has radius of order g. Under CMP 109's absolute radii the same step gives O(g²); that is not the model of
+record.) (iii) THE LARGE-FIELD PART carries exp(−2(1 + β₀)⁻¹p₀(g)) (LFR II (1.89)), super-polynomially small. So
+ρ(γ) = sup_{g ≤ γ}[C′(log g^{−2})^{−2q₁} + C₂e^{−cp₀(g)}] → 0, and ρ ≤ c, c − ρ > 0 for γ small. The bound uses only Theorem 1's
+inductive bounds at step k, which hold uniformly over every history with all couplings in ]0, γ]; so it is uniform over the
+window, which is what the sequence lemma's hypothesis quantifies over. This replaces, at the strength the sequence lemma needs, the
+smoothness clause Bałaban asserts on CMP 109 p. 264 and defers to a separate paper.
+
