+## 7. Added 2026-10-29, 11:4x EDT: (B1) proved on paper, and the symmetry clause it needs
+
+THE PROOF (ledger §109). (B1) holds on the class 𝒮(γ₀, κ, M) of gauge-invariant lattice actions with the following properties:
+their minimum is at the trivial configuration; their kernels are analytic in a strip of width κ with bound M; the quadratic part is
+elliptic (Δ ≥ γ₀p̂² on transverse modes); they are invariant under the lattice translations, rotations and reflections; and the
+dimension-four coefficient is Wilson's.
+
+The lattice Ward identities at leading degree, with the symmetry, fix the leading propagator and vertices to the continuum
+Yang–Mills ones. The inversion x ↦ −x removes every term of odd dimension. A hypercubic quadratic form annihilating gradients is
+a(p²δ − p⊗p). The homogeneous solutions for the cubic and quartic vertices vanish. No classification theorem is used.
+
+In background Feynman gauge the ghost, the gauge-fixing vertices and the Haar density do not depend on S, so c(S) − c_W is a
+difference of gluon loops. Split the loop momentum at k₀ and subtract each half's zero-momentum value. The ultraviolet half is
+bounded. The infrared half carries the continuum logarithm 8b₀ ln(1/p). The remainders gain two powers of momentum and contribute
+bounded constants.
+
+So §3's "to write out in full" and §6's "the leading-term identification of (B1)" are discharged at the strength of a proof on
+paper; the Lean kernel has not checked it.
+
+THE CLAUSE THE PROOF FOUND. Invariance under the lattice rotations and reflections is load-bearing. Without it a one-loop
+Chern–Simons-type term iε_{μνρσ}n_σP_ρ is allowed in Π(P): it is gauge invariant, transverse, and permitted by translation
+invariance alone. It would put a 1/P into β(P), and c(S) would not exist.
+
+The averaging of CMP 95 lacks this symmetry: its blocks are based at a corner and its contours run along the axes in a fixed order
+((1.6)–(1.7)). CMP 109 replaced that averaging to obtain the symmetry: centred blocks, L odd, and every shortest contour generated
+by permutations of the coordinate directions ((0.3)–(0.4), pp. 252–253). So (B2)'s clause (d) is read there. S_k ≥ 0 = S_k(1)
+gives (a). CMP 102 gives the analytic, uniformly bounded kernels. The classical scaling gives Wilson's dimension-four coefficient,
+the centred average having symbol 1 + O(P²).
+
+WHAT LEMMA B NOW RESTS ON: one page reading. CMP 95's ellipticity bound γ₀⟨∂₁B, ∂₁B⟩ ≤ ⟨B, Δ_kB⟩ is read for that paper's
+averaging; its transfer to CMP 109's symmetric averaging remains to be read.
