+**16.45 THE FULL STAR DECIMATION EXISTS IN ℤ³ AND NOT IN ℤ⁴ (08:1x EDT; the plan seat's item (3), first desk result).** THE CELL: the eight links at a site `x` (the STAR), plaquette-connected as the cross-polytope's 24 edges (two links at `x` share a plaquette iff they are not collinear); its footprint is the 24 plaquettes with corner `x`, each holding exactly two star links. A star leaves no lonely plaquette, so the skeleton's interaction through it begins at order `β³` (the three faces of an elementary cube at `x`: `∫ χ(U F₁ V†) χ(V F₂ W†) χ(W F₃ U†) = χ(F₁F₂F₃)/2`, coefficient `2a_½³` on the hexagon of far links), against order `β²` for the single-link cell (two plaquettes through one link). A design of stars alone is a set `S` meeting every plaquette in exactly one corner. In `ℤ³`, `S = {x ≡ (0,0,0) or (1,1,1) mod 2}` is one: every unit square in every plane has exactly one corner there (the control). **THEOREM S: in `ℤ⁴` no such `S` exists.** Proof. Let `S_t = {y ∈ ℤ³ : (y, t) ∈ S}`. A plaquette in a plane `(a, 4)` at `(y, t)` has corners `(y, t), (y + e_a, t), (y, t + 1), (y + e_a, t + 1)`, so every edge `{y, y + e_a}` of `ℤ³` has exactly one endpoint in `S_t ⊎ S_{t+1}`. Hence `S_t ∩ S_{t+1} = ∅`, and `S_t ∪ S_{t+1}` meets every edge of the connected bipartite graph `ℤ³` exactly once: it is a parity class, and `S_t` lies inside one parity class. But `S_t` meets every plaquette of `ℤ³` in exactly one corner, and a plaquette's two corners of one parity are a diagonal. The sites `0, e₁ + e₂, e₁ + e₃` (for the odd class `e₁, e₂, e₃`) are pairwise such diagonals, of the plaquettes `(0; 1,2)`, `(0; 1,3)`, `(e₁; 2,3)`, and no subset of a triangle meets each of its three pairs exactly once. ∎ A star with one link removed has the same footprint (each of the removed link's plaquettes keeps its other star link), so seven-link stars tile `ℤ⁴` no better.
