+**16.65-b THE DRESSING LEMMA'S PROOF HAD A HOLE AT ITS INDUCTIVE STEP; THE STATEMENT STANDS BY THE YANG–MILLS SEAT'S REPAIR WITH A SMALLER CONSTANT, AND THE REPAIRED NUMBER IS MET ON TWO ROADS: `β* = 0.4378` AT `γ = 6`, `0.4952` AT `γ = 5/2` (15:5x EDT; the second reader's finding 15:0x–15:2x, its ledger §119; `g52_reach.c` f560747e…, records `g52_reach_modeP.2026-10-29c.txt` 62f3b6be… and `…d.txt` 98447d6d…).** THE HOLE, confirmed on this page's own object. 16.65's proof writes a decoration as first-level pieces `{Q_i}` each carrying a decoration of itself "relative to `U ∪ Q_i`" and closes with "by induction `D_rel(Q_i) ≤ e^{κ*|Q_i|}`" — the lemma applied to a PIECE as base. The site count that step needs is (W3)'s `≤ 2|·|` single walls, and (W3) is a statement about a CLOSED base: it counts each wall four times, once per link, because every link of a wall lies in the base at multiplicity `≥ 2`. A wall rooted on a piece `Q` — one link in `Q ∖ U`, the other three anywhere in the earlier base — is counted ONCE among `Q`'s pairs, so the sites of a piece are up to `Σ_{m_Q(l) ≥ 2}(6 − m_Q(l)) ≤ 8|Q|`. The witness: the `2 × 2` disc has four interior links of multiplicity 2 in `Q`, each with four free plaquettes through it, and on a rest holding their outer links every one is a wall site — `16 = 4|Q|` against the `2|Q| = 8` the induction used. A second gap in the same sentence: "a set of first-level pieces each carrying a decoration of itself" misses a piece admissible relative to the union of two first-level pieces and to neither alone, and a closed piece reached only through later ones. THE REPAIR (the Yang–Mills seat's, read here and signed): a canonical sequential decomposition — `Q_j` the closing output from the smallest plaquette of the remainder adjacent to `U ∪ Q_1 ∪ … ∪ Q_{j−1}`, relative to that base — makes every link off `U` FIRST-OWNED by a piece of size `≥ 2` with multiplicity `≥ 2` there (a piece's multiplicity-1 links lie in the earlier base; a wall owns nothing); a piece's children attach only at its links of multiplicity `≥ 2`, at most `P(s) = 4·min(2s, s(s−1)/2) = 4, 12, 24, 40` then `8s` sites, since two plaquettes share at most one link and `Σ m = 4s` (the `s = 2` value exact: the only piece of size two is two plaquettes on one link, four free plaquettes through it); `U`'s children sit at its `8|U|` roots ((W2)); and THE WALLS ARE PAID ONCE, GLOBALLY: with `Δ′` the pieces of size `≥ 2`, `U ∪ Δ′` is closed and every wall has its four links in `L(U ∪ Δ′)`, so the walls are a subset of `walls(U ∪ Δ′) ≤ 2(|U| + |Δ′|)` by (W3) on that closed set — the factor four recovered where it is true. Then `Δ′ ↦ (sites, sizes, closing words)` is injective and the sum over all such data is `T^{8|U|}` with `T = 1 + Σ_{s≥2} 5^{s−1} z_s e^{2z_1 s} T^{P(s)}`, and `T ≤ e^λ` whenever `Σ_{s≥2} 5^{s−1} z_s e^{2z_1 s} e^{P(s)λ} ≤ λ` (iterate from `T_0 = 1`: `T_{n+1} ≤ 1 + λ ≤ e^λ`). RESULT: `D(U) ≤ e^{κ_U|U|}` with `κ_U = 2z_1 + κ′`, `κ′` the least root of `κ′ = 8 Σ_{s≥2} 5^{s−1} max(z_surf(s), z_br(s)) e^{2z_1 s} e^{P(s)κ′/8}`; 16.65-a's `κ*` satisfies `κ_U ≤ κ* − 2z_1(e^{κ*} − 1)`, so the page's constant is valid — its statement true by this proof — and the repaired one smaller. Law 18: the statement stands, the proof is replaced; the inequality of 16.65-a is kept on the page as what it is, a TRUE bound with a false proof superseded here. THE TWO ROADS UNDER THE REPAIR. `g52` carries the tree recursion as a mode (`G52_DRESS=tree`; integer spins in `z_1` and `z_surf` as in 16.74-a; `P(s)` as above; the Yang–Mills seat's certificate on the other side, its ledger §119 record b): at `γ = 6, μ = 5, F_0 = 20`: **`β* = 0.437960`** — feasible at the grid point 0.4378, infeasible at 0.4380, the certificate's digit; at `γ = 5/2`: **`0.495323`** — feasible at 0.4952, infeasible at 0.4954, the certificate's digit; the dressing root at `(0.4378, α = 0.0030, γ = 6)` reads `κ_U = 0.2078` against the seat's float road's 0.207. With `N_c(22) = 46,816` from 16.76 (one road until g48's F = 22 census ends): `F_0 = 22`: 0.442335 at `γ = 6`, 0.505972 at `γ = 5/2`; `μ = 4.33` (measured, not proved): 0.546148 / 0.556321. Under 16.65-a's recursion with the same `N_c(22)` (record d): 0.428872 at `γ = 6, F_0 = 22`, the other cells as in 16.74-a. The repaired number at `γ = 6, F_0 = 20` is the one that stands on proved inputs plus S12's remainder — the lattice gap at strong coupling of `SU(2)` on `ℤ⁴` to `β = 0.4378`, in the trunk's words; nothing is claimed above `β = 0.2452` until the trunk's criterion is met in full.
