+**Corrections first (law 18).** §23's closing paragraph is withdrawn in two places. (i) Its vertex graphs were written as simple; in the double D_Y a plaquette and its copy share the same two rest links at a sheet vertex, so the double's vertex graphs carry doubled edges, and the Yang–Mills seat's exact readings of the doubled stars (the doubled triangle 23/15, s = 1.0738; the doubled four-cycle 259/225, s = 1.0177; doubled trees exactly 1) put s_max above 1 — "if s_max ≤ 1" was not available as written. (ii) Σ_v|E(G_v)| = 4|D_Y| = 8|Y|, so √I(D_Y) ≤ s_max^{2|Y|}: the per-plaquette factor of the double is s_max², not s_max. Both defects die with the double itself, which the theorem below no longer needs.
+
+**Setting.** S the sheet (a closed branch-free surface at spin ½, F plaquettes, Euler characteristic χ), Y a piece (spin-1 plaquettes, S ∪ Y closed; T its rest links, O its off-S links, each off-S link in at least two plaquettes of Y), N(Y) := W(S∪Y)/((3a₁)^{|Y|}W(S)). For a vertex v let G_v be the vertex graph of S ∪ Y at v: its vertices the links of S ∪ Y at v, its edges the plaquettes at v, each edge coloured by its plaquette's spin (½ on S, 1 on Y). The sheet's star at v is a cycle C_v of d_v spin-½ edges through the sheet's links L_S(v); Y's star Γ_v is a graph of spin-1 edges on R_v ∪ O_v, R_v := L_S(v) ∩ {links of Y at v} the rest links Y uses at v (k_v := |R_v|), O_v the off-S links at v. **Γ_v is simple**: two distinct plaquettes at v span distinct pairs of links. For a graph G with edge spins, S(G) := ∫Π_{(i,j)∈E(G)} χ_{j_{ij}}(U_i^{−1}U_j) dU is its star integral.
+
+**Theorem L (the local bound).** For every piece Y,
+
+  |N(Y)| ≤ Π_{v : Γ_v ≠ ∅} r_v,  with  r_v² = S(Γ_v) off the sheet,  r_v² = 2^{k_v−2}·S(C^{½}_{k_v}(R_v) ∪ Γ_v) on it,
+
+where C^{½}_k(R_v) is the k-cycle of spin-½ edges on R_v in the sheet's cyclic order (for k = 1 a loop, χ_½(1) = 2, so that r_v² = S(Γ_v) there too; for k = 2 a doubled spin-½ edge).
+
+*Proof.* (i) Theorem V holds with mixed spins: for a closed complex D whose plaquettes carry spins j_q, I(D) := ∫Π_q χ_{j_q}(U_q) dU expands as in §23 with the Haar projector onto the invariants of ⊗_q V_{j_q} at every link; the characters are real, the contraction pairwise, and |I(D)| ≤ Π_v √S(G_v) with every edge of G_v carrying its plaquette's spin. (ii) W(S∪Y) = (2u)^F(3a₁)^{|Y|}·I(S∪Y) and W(S) = (2u)^F·2^{χ−F}, so N(Y) = I(S∪Y)/2^{χ−F}; and 2^{χ−F} = Π_{v∈S} 2^{1−d_v/2} = Π_{v∈S} √S(C^{½}_{d_v}) by the cycle law at spin ½ (the sheet's own vertex-norm bound is an equality). (iii) At a sheet vertex with Γ_v = ∅ the numerator's factor is √S(C_{d_v}) and cancels. At a sheet vertex with Γ_v ≠ ∅, the sheet links at v outside R_v have degree two in G_v and no spin-1 edge; integrating them, an arc of a sheet plaquettes between consecutive rest links contributes 2^{1−a}χ_½ of the two rest links' relative rotation, so S(G_v) = 2^{k_v−d_v}·S(C^{½}_{k_v}(R_v) ∪ Γ_v), and dividing by S(C_{d_v}) = 2^{2−d_v} gives r_v². Off the sheet G_v = Γ_v. ∎
+
+**What it removes.** No double, no cut constant C(T), no channel factor: a multiply-used rest link is a vertex of higher degree in Γ_v and nothing else; the doubled stars of §23 never enter, because every G_v is simple. The global Cauchy–Schwarz of J4 (sheet against piece) is replaced by one Frobenius inequality per vertex, with the sheet's star integrated exactly at each.
